74:Authoring is `look` / `run` → `fact` → `claim` → `prose` → `render`, with `target` alongside for
91:| `design.md` | the rendered document — prose plus evidence rows, plain markdown so agents read it, tooling greps it, and review happens in a diff |
96:Markdown is what `render` emits, not what tetel is about. **The render target is the most replaceable
118:| **discharge** | the claim asserting a premise holds at the destination. `render --out` refuses a document with a premise nothing answers |
3:All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e2 ("ci: make the guards authoritative for the repository, not just for one editor"); at the start of authoring the working tree was clean, and the memo you are reading plus its snapshot are the one addition this workspace makes to it. TET-28 asks for a refusal: a memo that tells an implementer to modify a symbol must carry a repo-wide census of that symbol, captured by the tool rather than typed, or the memo does not render. The motivating defect, as the ticket reports it from a private project's review trail and described here only by its shape: a memo recommended modifying a function and spent a repo-wide negative about its callers, while the author's actual caller sweep — honest in its own fact — was scoped to a single file; per the ticket, one workspace-rooted grep would have landed on a second production gate with a different mechanism, and the miss instead survived five review rounds before both attack passes found it independently. This memo settles the two questions the ticket leaves open — what the trigger is and whether it is decidable, and what "workspace-rooted" means decidably — and reaches a design that keeps the refusal format-level.
35:What is decidable is a declaration. A modification target becomes a structured record — a symbol string plus a citation of the fact that censuses it — created by a new authoring verb and rendered as its own section, the way the evidence ledger and Facts table already render structured records without generating prose from fields. The refusal then never reads English at all: it fires when a declared target's cited fact carries no qualifying census entry, which is an objective contradiction between the document and the record it claims to rest on — the exact principle the machine partition already states for provenance drift, and the README's own example of the one decidable refusal shape, "you did not point at your evidence". The disposition follows the project's report-versus-refuse discriminator from the other side: the note-outside-extent check stayed human-owed because only a person can tell context from conclusion, but for a declared-and-uncensused target nothing is left to tell — the remedy is singular. Run the workspace-rooted grep for that symbol, mint the fact, cite it; or withdraw the target.
39:Declaration moves the vacuity question to under-declaration, and the honest answer is that no refusal can reach it: deciding that a memo recommended something it never declared is the same heuristic read of prose the trigger just rejected. The design's answer is visibility rather than detection, in two places. First, the section never disappears: once a workspace declares any target the section renders, and once any claim exists it renders even when empty, as "Modification targets: none declared" — so a memo full of tell-the-implementer language with an empty census section shows its hole to every reader, instead of an undeclared target being an invisible omission. Second, check's standing non-coverage list — whose first entry is already "dependents that never declared themselves" — gains the census's own blind spot, stated as such. The ticket offers a further reason to assess the risk as small: its review protocol's adversarial pass puts every tell-the-implementer claim in scope unconditionally. That is a property of an agent definition outside this repository, not of tetel, so this design records it as the ticket's premise and deliberately does not lean on it — the rendered hole and the printed blind spot carry the visibility argument on their own, for any consumer of the tool, with or without that protocol around it.
43:The refusal lives at the earliest surface that can decide it: declaration. `tetel target` refuses to create a target whose cited fact has no qualifying census entry — the same authoring-time shape as `tetel fact` refusing on an empty pending buffer. A fact's extent is immutable once minted and a target immutable once declared, so this one refusal makes "every declared target is censused" a workspace invariant, and render needs no refusal of its own for workspace-authored documents: its job is to emit the section. What render cannot vouch for, check re-verifies — the rendered document against its own snapshot, reported as [uncensused-target] in the machine partition's scope line — which is what catches a hand-authored or tampered document that never passed through the refusing verb. Like the other snapshot-dependent findings, that check runs only when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure. The verb itself must be one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, the pattern look and fact already use, because this render path has already recorded one CLI/MCP drift on a duplicated act.
55:check's report already carries a standing non-coverage list, printed item by item, and this feature adds its own blind spots to it rather than leaving them implicit. A textual census cannot see dynamic dispatch, re-exports or aliased imports, or callers assembled by macros and string concatenation. Its traversal is the system grep's — tetel shells out precisely to avoid owning a subtly different regex dialect — so the platform's rules for symlinked directories and the binary-file skip bound what a repo-wide search physically visited. Rooting establishes where the search started, never which tree it should have started in: whether the censused root is the tree the memo designs against remains a reader's question, answerable from the per-root tree-state report the check already prints. Whether anyone read the census is the insincere-examination ceiling: a census is a list of sites, not an understanding of them. And under-declared targets are invisible by construction — the hole in the rendered section and the non-coverage line are their only trace.
65:The ticket asks that this ship before any TET-27 co-evolution run, on the argument that findings the census would have surfaced must not be double-credited to a prototype, which would make that run's "would existing machinery have caught this anyway" kill-arm unevaluable. The premise of that argument lives in the tickets and the private review trail, not in this repository, so this memo does not grade it; what this memo can establish is that the sequencing costs nothing. The feature's surface is one recorded entry in the grep path, two serde-defaulted fields on the extent record, one new record type and verb, one rendered section, one shared refusal, and one machine check with its printed limits — each an extension of a mechanism cited in this memo's facts, and none of them a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation, the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject. Shipping first is therefore safe whether or not the double-crediting argument is right, and the design was not bent to accommodate it. What this memo deliberately does not decide: any implementation detail beyond the record shapes and predicates above, and any widening of the trigger — in particular, no future measurement of a mention-based advisory should be allowed to harden into a refusal, because the measured evidence in this repository says that detector cannot tell quotation from conclusion.
90:| C8 | Under-declaration — the memo recommends modifying a symbol it never declared — cannot be a refusal and must not be silently absorbed either. Deciding from prose that a sentence "tells an implementer to modify X" is a heuristic read of English, exactly the class the charter routes to the human-owed partition or nowhere; scope.rs's measured precedent shows the symbol-detector that trigger would need flags quotation and misses conclusion. The design therefore makes the omission visible instead of detected: (a) once a workspace declares any target, render emits a Modification targets section, and once any claim exists it emits the section even when empty — "Modification targets: none declared" — so a memo that recommends changes while declaring nothing shows a hole where the census should be, in front of every reader; and (b) check's standing non-coverage list, whose first entry is already "dependents that never declared themselves", gains a census-specific line ("a recommendation whose target was never declared") so the blind spot is printed rather than implicit. | OWED |
91:| C9 | Where the refusal lives: at declaration, then re-verified at check. The `target` verb refuses to create a target whose cited fact carries no qualifying census entry — the same authoring-time shape as fact refusing on an empty pending buffer ("a fact needs a preceding look/run"; a target needs a preceding rooted census). Because a fact's extent is immutable once minted and a target is immutable once declared, this makes "every declared target is censused" a workspace invariant, and no render-path refusal is needed for workspace-authored documents; render's job is only to emit the targets section. check independently re-verifies the rendered document against its snapshot and reports [uncensused-target] as a machine failure in the partition's scope line, which is what catches a hand-authored or tampered document that never passed through the refusing verb. Like notes_outside_extent and mint_windows, that check runs only when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure. The verb must be one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, the pattern look and fact already use, because this codebase has recorded a CLI/MCP drift on exactly such a duplicated act. | OWED |
95:| C13 | The feature's implementation surface is small: one additional recorded entry in look_grep, two serde-defaulted structured fields on the extent entry (following world_root's own compatibility precedent), one new append-only record type whose authoring verb refuses uncensused declarations from one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, one rendered section beside the existing ledger and Facts tables, and one machine check with its human-owed and non-coverage lines. Each component extends a mechanism this memo's facts describe, and none of them is a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation — the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject — so shipping this first imposes no design debt on that exploration. | OWED |
108:| F4 | How a memo is assembled and what render refuses today. compose.rs render(): prose blocks in order, then an appended "## Evidence ledger" table (one row per non-withdrawn claim, Status always OWED, no Domain/Extent columns — v1's Domain ⊆ Extent was retired on evidence and deriving scope from facts' extents "would recreate exactly the vacuity that measurement rejected"), then a "## Facts" table (ID, Note, Extent-as-captured labels, Rests under). Structured tables rendered from fields are the established shape; only labels are rendered, never absolute keys ("a committed document should not carry /Users/<someone>/..."). main.rs Render arm (lines 640-700): compose::render, then write document, then snapshot::write (document first so a failed snapshot leaves a recoverable state), then a pending-observations warning that is explicitly "Warned, never refused: an author may have deliberately looked at something they chose not to cite". Nothing in the render path refuses on the content of the workspace today. One drift precedent recorded in the comments: the CLI arm used to mint the workspace identity and the MCP render handler did not, so a memo authored over MCP shipped without one — the fix moved the act into snapshot::write, the one shared place. | /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs; /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs lines 640-700 | C9, C13 |
