2:{"event":"Create","id":"P2","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e2 ("ci: make the guards authoritative for the repository, not just for one editor"), clean working tree. TET-28 asks for a refusal: a memo that recommends modifying a symbol must carry a repo-wide census of that symbol, captured by the tool, or the memo does not render. This memo settles two questions the ticket leaves open — what the trigger is and whether it is decidable, and what "workspace-rooted" means decidably — and reaches a design that keeps the refusal format-level.","cite":["C1"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786130606}
9:{"event":"Create","id":"P9","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"What is decidable is a declaration. A modification target becomes a structured record — a symbol string plus a citation of the fact that censuses it — created by a new authoring verb and rendered as its own section, the way the evidence ledger and Facts table already render structured records without generating prose from fields. The refusal then never reads English at all: it fires when a declared target's cited fact carries no qualifying census entry, which is an objective contradiction between the document and the record it claims to rest on — the exact principle the machine partition already states for provenance drift, and the README's own example of the one decidable refusal shape, "you did not point at your evidence". The disposition follows the project's report-versus-refuse discriminator from the other side: the note-outside-extent check stayed human-owed because only a person can tell context from conclusion, but for a declared-and-uncensused target nothing is left to tell — the remedy is singular. Run the workspace-rooted grep for that symbol, mint the fact, cite it; or withdraw the target.","cite":["C7"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786131246}
10:{"event":"Create","id":"P10","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"Declaration moves the vacuity question to under-declaration, and the honest answer is that no refusal can reach it: deciding that a memo recommended something it never declared is the same heuristic read of prose the trigger just rejected. The design's answer is visibility rather than detection, in two places. First, the section never disappears: once a workspace declares any target the section renders, and once any claim exists it renders even when empty, as "Modification targets: none declared" — so a memo full of tell-the-implementer language with an empty census section shows its hole to every reader, instead of an undeclared target being an invisible omission. Second, check's standing non-coverage list — whose first entry is already "dependents that never declared themselves" — gains the census's own blind spot, stated as such. The ticket also argues under-declaration is caught because the adversarial-review pass puts every tell-the-implementer claim in scope unconditionally; that is true of the review protocol this project runs around tetel, but it is a property of an agent definition outside this repository, so this design records it as the ticket's premise and deliberately does not lean on it: the rendered hole and the printed blind spot carry the visibility argument on their own.","cite":["C8"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786131258}
11:{"event":"Create","id":"P11","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"The refusal lives in both surfaces that can see the workspace or its snapshot, because each covers the other's blind spot. render --out refuses — the render path's first content-conditional refusal, and deliberately so: its existing pending-observations warning stays a warning because only the author can tell a deliberate uncited look from a forgotten one, but an uncensused declared target has no honest reading to protect. That refusal must be implemented once, in a dispatch shared by CLI and MCP — this exact path has already drifted once, when workspace identity was minted by the CLI arm and not the MCP handler until the act moved into snapshot::write. check then re-verifies the rendered document against its snapshot and reports [uncensused-target] in the machine partition's scope line, which catches a hand-carried or stale document that never went through the refusing render. Like the other snapshot-dependent findings, the check can only run when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure.","cite":["C9"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786131269}
16:{"event":"Create","id":"P16","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"check's report already carries a standing non-coverage list, printed item by item, and this feature adds its own blind spots to it rather than leaving them implicit. A textual census cannot see dynamic dispatch, re-exports or aliased imports, or callers assembled by macros and string concatenation. Its traversal is the system grep's — tetel shells out precisely to avoid owning a subtly different regex dialect — so the platform's rules for symlinked directories and the binary-file skip bound what a repo-wide search physically visited. Rooting establishes where the search started, never which tree it should have started in: whether the censused root is the tree the memo designs against remains a reader's question, answerable from the per-root tree-state report the check already prints. Whether anyone read the census is the insincere-examination ceiling. And under-declared targets are invisible by construction — the hole in the rendered section and the non-coverage line are their only trace. A reviewer who wants the census to mean more than "a repo-wide list of textual match sites exists" must read it, which is exactly the reading the Facts table was built to concentrate.","cite":["C12"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786131443}
18:{"event":"Create","id":"P18","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"The ticket asks that this ship before any TET-27 co-evolution run, on the argument that findings the census would have surfaced must not be double-credited to a prototype, which would make that run's "would existing machinery have caught this anyway" kill-arm unevaluable. The premise of that argument lives in the tickets and the private review trail, not in this repository, so this memo does not grade it; what this memo can establish is that the sequencing costs nothing. The feature's surface is one recorded entry in the grep path, two serde-defaulted fields on the extent record, one new record type and verb, one rendered section, one shared refusal, and one machine check with its printed limits — and none of it touches or presupposes TET-27's subject matter: no code-to-graph links, no commit references, no dependency propagation. Shipping first is therefore safe whether or not the double-crediting argument is right, and the design was not bent to accommodate it. What this memo deliberately does not decide: any implementation detail beyond the record shapes and predicates above, and any widening of the trigger — in particular, no future measurement of a mention-based advisory should be allowed to harden into a refusal, because the measured evidence in this repository says that detector cannot tell quotation from conclusion.","cite":["C13","C6"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786131458}
