3:All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 67191f5 ("tet28: pin the census refusal, and make the parity guard real"); the working tree was clean at the start of authoring, and this memo plus its snapshot are the one addition the workspace makes to it. That commit matters beyond pinning: it means TET-28's modification-target machinery — the record type, the refusing verb, the render section, the snapshot re-verification — is in the tree, and this design reads it as implemented precedent rather than as a sibling design document. TET-29 asks for a second required section: any design that transplants a mechanism from another site must carry a premise inventory — donor site, the donor's comment-stated premises transcribed verbatim, and per premise a claim that it holds at the destination — with `check` refusing the shape (section absent, or a premise row citing no claim) and never judging the content. This memo settles what the trigger is, what the record holds, what "transcribed verbatim" can decidably mean, where the refusals fire, and what is printed as uncatchable.
7:The answer, up front. The ticket survives contact with the codebase, but not in its own vocabulary — four reformulations, each argued below. First, the trigger: "any design that transplants" is as undecidable from prose as "a memo's recommendation" was for TET-28, so the trigger is an author-declared transplant record, and the refusal attaches to declarations. Second, and largest: "transcribed verbatim" cannot be a typed field on trust — a premise is refused unless its text is a verbatim substring of one observation's captured output inside the cited donor fact, which converts transcription from a request into a machine guarantee and makes TET-29 the first load-bearing use of the README's supporting-span-selection direction. Third, the refusal the ticket placed at `check` splits across three surfaces: quotation invariants refuse at the verb, completeness refuses at `render --out`, and `check` re-verifies both against the snapshot for documents that never passed through either. Fourth, a composition the ticket left open: the transplant's destination is a citation of a live modification target, so declaring a transplant forces TET-28's census at the landing site. What none of this touches — deliberately — is content: the tool never decides what counts as a premise, whether a selection is deep enough, or whether a discharge claim is true. Its entire effect is to move the violated property from "asserted by no claim at all" into the evidence ledger, where the existing grounding loop can reach it.
13:The ticket's rule as written — "any design or fix that transplants a mechanism from another site must carry a premise-inventory section" — has the same undecidable subject TET-28's ticket had. Nothing in the record model types anything as a transplant; deciding that a fix transplants means either reading the prose for tell-tale language or comparing code across two sites, and both are heuristic reads of content, the class this project has measured dead once (scope.rs's mention-detector flagged a legitimate quotation and missed both real overreaches) and has now shipped a refusal-shaped alternative to once (targets.rs, whose module doc carries the argument as implemented doctrine, not proposal). So the trigger is the same shape: the author declares a transplant as a structured record, the refusal attaches to the declaration, and the memo that transplants without declaring is not detected — it is made visible, by a section that renders its own absence and a non-coverage line that prints the blind spot. This memo does not re-litigate that reasoning; it verifies it transfers, and it does: the one disanalogy — a transplant is an act the design performs, where a modification target is a thing the design names — changes nothing about decidability, because in both cases the undecidable step is deciding from content that the memo is in the rule's scope at all.
23:Two objections were the reason this could not simply be assumed, and both were settled against the capture code rather than by preference. First, strictness: a donor comment wrapped across lines, with `//` or `*` prefixes and indentation inside it, is in fact a contiguous byte substring of a `look` capture. The plain open stores the file's bytes verbatim — no line numbers, no headers, no reflowing — and the `--lines` mode stores the selected lines re-joined with newlines, normalising CRLF endings and the trailing newline in that mode only; in both modes the relation is defined over the captured string itself, so what a memo can quote is exactly what was captured, never a third spelling. The cost is that the premise appears in the rendered section with its comment syntax intact, which the "never paraphrase" constraint treats as a feature: the reader sees the donor's clause as the donor's file spells it. Stripping the noise would require per-language comment knowledge, the exact coupling scope.rs's "Language-agnostic by construction" section exists to exclude. The two capture modes that genuinely fight containment — grep outputs, whose lines carry `lineno:` prefixes, and whole-search records, whose output is empty — make `look` of the donor file the natural donor capture, and the refusal's diagnostic should say so; the relation itself stays indifferent to observation kind. Whether the motivating premise itself clears this bar is not left to argument — the next paragraph tests it at the source. Second, soundness: a fact's output is the join of its observations' outputs, boundaries unstored, so whole-output containment would accept text straddling the seam between two captures — bytes never contiguous in anything the author saw. The fix is one serde-defaulted per-entry byte-length field on the extent entry, the same compatibility move that added kind and pattern for TET-28: boundaries become reconstructible, the relation tightens to "substring of a single observation's output", and a donor fact minted by an older build is ineligible with the same cheap remedy — look again.
37:## Where the refusals fire: verb, then render --out, then check
39:TET-28 moved its whole refusal to the verb and left render refusal-free. TET-29 cannot copy that placement wholesale, and the reason is a real disanalogy, not taste: a census either exists when the target is declared or it does not, but a premise inventory is built across time. The instrument works by transcribe-first — the author selects the donor's premises before knowing whether each holds at the destination; that confrontation is the mechanism. Refusing an undischarged premise at declaration would demand the destination-holds claim before the premise can exist, inverting the order into answer-first-transcribe-after, and pushing an author who cannot yet discharge a premise toward the quiet exit of never declaring it — the exact "refusing would make the honest case unwritable" failure the project's own discriminator names. So the refusals split by question. The verb refuses everything that is per-record and decidable at creation: a donor fact that does not exist or predates span boundaries, a premise that is not a genuine quotation, a destination that is not a live target, a discharge naming no live claim. Completeness — every premise on every live transplant discharged — is a property of the finished document, and it is refused at `render --out`: the act that writes the reviewable artifact and its snapshot, under the already-pinned policy that a rendered memo is never hand-edited. A memo with a declared-but-unexamined premise fails to come into existence rather than existing and failing; the remedy is singular; a preview render still assembles with undischarged rows marked loudly, so mid-work visibility survives. This makes TET-29 the first refusal on the render path, which is the design's most debatable departure — the alternative of letting the memo render and having only check fail it matches the ticket's letter, but it manufactures exactly the artifact the ticket exists to prevent and then depends on the author running check. Last, check re-verifies the whole shape against the shipped snapshot, both directions since tampering goes both ways, as a machine failure beside uncensused targets; containment is re-checked byte-for-byte from the facts.jsonl the snapshot already ships, so check still runs nothing and fetches nothing. Without a snapshot, transplant rows are human-owed unverifiable — the standing choice for every snapshot-dependent finding.
45:The rendered section sits beside the modification-target section and follows its conventions: labels rather than absolute keys, withdrawn records dropped, and — once the workspace has any claim — an empty section that renders as "none declared" rather than disappearing, because the hole is the finding and an absent section would make the same omission invisible. Per live transplant, the reader sees the donor fact and its extent labels, the destination target, and each premise's selected bytes verbatim in a fenced block with the discharging claim's id beside it. One shipped rule is deliberately inverted here: compose.rs refuses to inline captured output because output is unbounded, but a premise is an author-bounded selection, and inlining it is the entire point — the reader must see the donor's clause on the page, spelled the way the donor's file spells it, next to the claim that answers it, or the section degenerates into opaque pointers. Fenced blocks rather than table cells, because a premise legitimately spans lines and a markdown cell cannot carry multi-line text without escaping it, which would break the byte-fidelity the whole design is built on.
59:Every component extends a mechanism already in the tree, and none crosses a doctrinal line: one serde-defaulted per-entry output-length field on the extent record (the compatibility move that added kind and pattern for TET-28, with the same absent-means-ineligible reading — a legacy donor fact is re-looked, never guessed about); one new record kind, counter, and id prefix; a transplants.jsonl written by one CLI/MCP-shared dispatch and shipped by one new line in the snapshot's deliberately-enumerated file list, whose targets.jsonl entry already documents why a record log must ship; one rendered section; one completeness refusal on the render --out path; one snapshot re-verification beside the census one; and the six non-coverage lines. Nothing parses any programming language — the containment check is byte comparison, indifferent to what the bytes mean — nothing is re-run or fetched at check time, and no score or document-level verdict appears anywhere. What this memo deliberately does not decide: the premise-row id spelling and event grammar beyond append-only-with-withdraw; whether span selection should later generalise to ordinary mint-time notes, which stays a Direction item with this feature as its first measurement; and any widening of the trigger toward detecting undeclared transplants, which the measured evidence against prose-reading refusals rules out now for the same reason it did for TET-28.
78:| C2 | TET-29's trigger must be a declaration, not a detection, by the same argument TET-28 implemented rather than merely designed. "Any design or fix that transplants a mechanism from another site" denotes no enumerable set of records: nothing in the record model types a claim or prose block as a transplant, and deciding from English (or from comparing code across two sites) that a fix "transplants" is a heuristic read — the class scope.rs measured dead for symbol mentions ("Naming a symbol the code you read calls is normal; concluding about what that symbol *does* is the failure — and those two are not distinguishable by string matching") and the class targets.rs's shipped doc comment rules out as a refusal trigger. So a transplant is a record the author declares, the refusal attaches to the declaration, and under-declaration is handled the way TET-28 handles it: an always-rendered section that shows its hole, plus a printed non-coverage line, never a detector. | OWED |
80:| C4 | The strictness objections to byte-level containment dissolve on the actual capture path, and every weaker relation reopens the invention channel. A donor comment wrapped across lines with interior prefix noise (`//`, `*`, indentation) IS a contiguous byte substring of a look_path capture: the plain open stores the file's bytes verbatim, and the --lines mode stores the selected lines re-joined with "
" (normalising CRLF endings and the trailing newline in that mode only) — in both modes the premise relation is defined over the captured String itself, so what a memo can quote is exactly what was captured, never a third spelling. The cost is only that the selected premise carries its comment syntax into the rendered section, which the "never paraphrase" constraint endorses rather than tolerates. Stripping that noise would require knowing each language's comment syntax, which is precisely the hard-coded per-language coupling scope.rs's own "Language-agnostic by construction" doctrine section excludes ("There is no list of languages and no list of extensions"; the rejected alternative, a hard-coded extension-to-language map, is "exactly the coupling this avoids"). Two capture modes genuinely fight containment — grep-derived match outputs interleave "lineno:" prefixes between lines, and whole-search/no-match records carry empty output — so the design's guidance is that a donor fact is minted from `look` of the donor file; that is guidance about which observation to select from, not a new rule, since the relation is indifferent to which kind produced the bytes. Weaker relations fail: per-line containment lets an author stitch non-adjacent lines into a sentence the donor never wrote, and whitespace- or case-normalised matching means the memo shows text the capture does not contain — both are paraphrase with extra steps. | OWED |
85:| C9 | The refusals land on three surfaces, each the earliest that can decide its own question. (1) At the verb: quotation invariants — donor fact exists and is span-eligible, premise text is a verbatim substring of a single observation's output, destination is a live target, a discharge names an existing live claim. These are per-record and decidable at creation, exactly like target's census refusal. (2) At `render --out`: completeness — a live transplant with an undischarged premise (none cited, or its discharge claim withdrawn) refuses the write. This is deliberately later than the verb (the intermediate state is legal while authoring) and deliberately earlier than check (the ticket's placement): --out is the act that writes the reviewable artifact and its snapshot under the pinned policy that a rendered memo is never hand-edited, so a memo with a declared-but-unexamined premise should fail to come into existence rather than exist and fail; the remedy is singular — discharge the premise, or withdraw it or its transplant with --why. A preview render (no --out) still assembles, marking undischarged rows loudly, so mid-work visibility is preserved; this render/--out asymmetry is new, and it is the design's most debatable departure. (3) At check: re-verification of everything above against the shipped snapshot — both directions, since tampering can go either way (a transplant row the snapshot lacks; a snapshot premise whose text is not contained in its donor fact's output; a premise whose discharge claim is withdrawn or missing) — as a machine failure in the same partition as uncensused targets, with rows in a snapshotless document reported human-owed as unverifiable, the standing choice for snapshot-dependent findings. Containment is re-checked byte-for-byte from facts.jsonl, which already ships full outputs; check runs nothing and fetches nothing to do it. | OWED |
87:| C11 | The rendered section follows compose.rs's shipped conventions with one deliberate difference. A "## Transplants" section renders beside the modification-target section: per live transplant, the donor fact id with its extent labels (labels, never absolute keys — the committed-document rule), the destination target id and symbol, and one entry per live premise carrying the selected text verbatim in a fenced block with the discharging claim id beside it; withdrawn transplants and premises are dropped, and once any claim exists the section renders even when empty ("none declared"), because the hole is the finding — the same visibility answer to under-declaration the target section shipped. The deliberate difference: compose.rs refuses to inline captured output into the document because output is unbounded, but a premise is an author-bounded selection, not a whole capture, so inlining it verbatim is both safe and the entire point — the reader must see the donor's clause on the page, spelled the way the donor's file spells it, or the section is a list of opaque pointers. Fenced blocks rather than table cells because a premise legitimately spans lines and markdown table cells cannot carry multi-line verbatim text without escaping, which would violate byte-fidelity. | OWED |
90:| C14 | The implementation surface is small and every piece extends a mechanism already in the tree: one serde-defaulted per-entry output-length field on ExtentEntry (the same compatibility precedent that added kind and pattern, with the same absent-means-ineligible reading); one new record kind with its counter and id prefix; one transplants.jsonl appended by one CLI/MCP-shared dispatch, shipped by adding one line to snapshot.rs's deliberately-enumerated SNAPSHOT_FILES (whose targets.jsonl entry already documents why a record log must ship: without it the refusal "would hold only inside the workspace that authored the memo"); one rendered section beside render_targets; one completeness refusal in the render --out path; one check re-verifying from files the snapshot already carries (transplant machine findings beside uncensused_targets, unverifiable rows human-owed without a snapshot); and the new NON_COVERAGE lines. Nothing parses any programming language, nothing is re-run at check time, and no aggregate, score, or document-level verdict is introduced anywhere. | OWED |
104:| F7 | The assembly-and-verification surfaces TET-29 extends, first-hand. compose.rs: render appends render_targets between the prose and the evidence ledger; the section renders "None declared..." once any claim exists ("the hole is the finding" — "an absent section would make the same omission invisible, which is the failure mode, not the fix"); withdrawn targets are dropped, live ones render as a two-column table \|`symbol`\|F#\|; only labels are rendered, never absolute keys ("a committed document should not carry /Users/<someone>/..."), and captured output is deliberately NOT rendered into the document ("unbounded ... it is in the snapshot for anyone who needs it, and a document that inlined it would bury the prose"). checks.rs: Findings has uncensused_targets as a MACHINE failure ("re-verified against the shipped snapshot ... what this catches is the document that never passed through that verb: hand-authored, edited after rendering, or carrying a target row its own snapshot does not have. Both directions are checked"), and unverifiable_targets as human-owed when no snapshot shipped; machine_check_failed() ORs grammar/subset/abutting/unsettled/cascade/ledger/verdict-disagreement/out-of-proof/uncensused-targets/provenance; out_of_proof (every record grades a wording the claim no longer carries) is a machine failure whose remedy is reproving. report.rs: exit codes 0/1/2; NON_COVERAGE is a const list printed item by item ("tetel does not catch: ..."), and TET-28 added five census entries to it with a comment explaining why ("a refusal that fired is easily read as coverage it never claimed"); first entry is "dependents that never declared themselves". | /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs lines 1-130; /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs lines 120-215; /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs lines 13-40 | C9, C11, C12, C13, C14 |
105:| F8 | snapshot.rs first-hand: SNAPSHOT_FILES is a deliberately enumerated list — "a future workspace file is a deliberate decision to ship or withhold, not an accident of cp -r" — currently [facts.jsonl, claims.jsonl, prose.jsonl, targets.jsonl, counters.json, pending.json, refusals.log, identity.json]. targets.jsonl's own comment states the pattern TET-29 must repeat: "Shipped, not withheld: without it check can see the rendered target rows but nothing to verify them against, and the census refusal would hold only inside the workspace that authored the memo — exactly the reviewer-of-a-committed-document case the snapshot exists for." So shipping a transplants.jsonl is one line in this array, and check then re-verifies premise containment from facts.jsonl (which carries full Create events including output) plus the transplant log. The module also pins the policy "a rendered memo is never hand-edited" and mints workspace identity in write() itself so no caller can forget it. | /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/snapshot.rs lines 55-145 | C9, C14 |
