1:{"event":"Create","id":"P1","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"# TET-29 design: the transplant premise inventory, and what "transcribed verbatim" must mean in a tool where evidence is captured

All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 67191f5 ("tet28: pin the census refusal, and make the parity guard real"); the working tree was clean at the start of authoring, and this memo plus its snapshot are the one addition the workspace makes to it. That commit matters beyond pinning: it means TET-28's modification-target machinery — the record type, the refusing verb, the render section, the snapshot re-verification — is in the tree, and this design reads it as implemented precedent rather than as a sibling design document. TET-29 asks for a second required section: any design that transplants a mechanism from another site must carry a premise inventory — donor site, the donor's comment-stated premises transcribed verbatim, and per premise a claim that it holds at the destination — with `check` refusing the shape (section absent, or a premise row citing no claim) and never judging the content. This memo settles what the trigger is, what the record holds, what "transcribed verbatim" can decidably mean, where the refusals fire, and what is printed as uncatchable.","cite":["C1"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786172352}
2:{"event":"Create","id":"P2","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"## The trigger: a declared transplant, for the reasons TET-28 already carries in its shipped code

The ticket's rule as written — "any design or fix that transplants a mechanism from another site must carry a premise-inventory section" — has the same undecidable subject TET-28's ticket had. Nothing in the record model types anything as a transplant; deciding that a fix transplants means either reading the prose for tell-tale language or comparing code across two sites, and both are heuristic reads of content, the class this project has measured dead once (scope.rs's mention-detector flagged a legitimate quotation and missed both real overreaches) and has now shipped a refusal-shaped alternative to once (targets.rs, whose module doc carries the argument as implemented doctrine, not proposal). So the trigger is the same shape: the author declares a transplant as a structured record, the refusal attaches to the declaration, and the memo that transplants without declaring is not detected — it is made visible, by a section that renders its own absence and a non-coverage line that prints the blind spot. This memo does not re-litigate that reasoning; it verifies it transfers, and it does: the one disanalogy — a transplant is an act the design performs, where a modification target is a thing the design names — changes nothing about decidability, because in both cases the undecidable step is deciding from content that the memo is in the rule's scope at all.","cite":["C2"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786172628}
4:{"event":"Create","id":"P4","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"Two objections were the reason this could not simply be assumed, and both were settled against the capture code rather than by preference. First, strictness: a donor comment wrapped across lines, with `//` or `*` prefixes and indentation inside it, is in fact a contiguous byte substring of a `look` capture, because look_path stores the file's bytes verbatim — no line numbers, no headers, no reflowing. The cost is that the premise appears in the rendered section with its comment syntax intact, which the "never paraphrase" constraint treats as a feature: the reader sees the donor's clause as the donor's file spells it. Stripping the noise would require per-language comment knowledge, the exact coupling the language-agnostic doctrine exists to exclude. The two capture modes that genuinely fight containment — grep outputs, whose lines carry `lineno:` prefixes, and whole-search records, whose output is empty — make `look` of the donor file the natural donor capture, and the refusal's diagnostic should say so; the relation itself stays indifferent to observation kind. In the motivating incident this cost is nil: the missed premise was a clause of a single comment sentence the author was already quoting into a fact note by hand. Second, soundness: a fact's output is the join of its observations' outputs, boundaries unstored, so whole-output containment would accept text straddling the seam between two captures — bytes never contiguous in anything the author saw. The fix is one serde-defaulted per-entry byte-length field on the extent entry, the same compatibility move that added kind and pattern for TET-28: boundaries become reconstructible, the relation tightens to "substring of a single observation's output", and a donor fact minted by an older build is ineligible with the same cheap remedy — look again.","cite":["C4","C5"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786172727}
6:{"event":"Create","id":"P6","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"## Where the refusals fire: verb, then render --out, then check

TET-28 moved its whole refusal to the verb and left render refusal-free. TET-29 cannot copy that placement wholesale, and the reason is a real disanalogy, not taste: a census either exists when the target is declared or it does not, but a premise inventory is built across time. The instrument works by transcribe-first — the author selects the donor's premises before knowing whether each holds at the destination; that confrontation is the mechanism. Refusing an undischarged premise at declaration would demand the destination-holds claim before the premise can exist, inverting the order into answer-first-transcribe-after, and pushing an author who cannot yet discharge a premise toward the quiet exit of never declaring it — the exact "refusing would make the honest case unwritable" failure the project's own discriminator names. So the refusals split by question. The verb refuses everything that is per-record and decidable at creation: a donor fact that does not exist or predates span boundaries, a premise that is not a genuine quotation, a destination that is not a live target, a discharge naming no live claim. Completeness — every premise on every live transplant discharged — is a property of the finished document, and it is refused at `render --out`: the act that writes the reviewable artifact and its snapshot, under the already-pinned policy that a rendered memo is never hand-edited. A memo with a declared-but-unexamined premise fails to come into existence rather than existing and failing; the remedy is singular; a preview render still assembles with undischarged rows marked loudly, so mid-work visibility survives. This makes TET-29 the first refusal on the render path, which is the design's most debatable departure — the alternative of letting the memo render and having only check fail it matches the ticket's letter, but it manufactures exactly the artifact the ticket exists to prevent and then depends on the author running check. Last, check re-verifies the whole shape against the shipped snapshot, both directions since tampering goes both ways, as a machine failure beside uncensused targets; containment is re-checked byte-for-byte from the facts.jsonl the snapshot already ships, so check still runs nothing and fetches nothing. Without a snapshot, transplant rows are human-owed unverifiable — the standing choice for every snapshot-dependent finding.","cite":["C8","C9"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786173050}
8:{"event":"Create","id":"P8","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"The answer, up front. The ticket survives contact with the codebase, but not in its own vocabulary — four reformulations, each argued below. First, the trigger: "any design that transplants" is as undecidable from prose as "a memo's recommendation" was for TET-28, so the trigger is an author-declared transplant record, and the refusal attaches to declarations. Second, and largest: "transcribed verbatim" cannot be a typed field on trust — a premise is refused unless its text is a verbatim substring of one observation's captured output inside the cited donor fact, which converts transcription from a request into a machine guarantee and makes TET-29 the first load-bearing use of the README's supporting-span-selection direction. Third, the refusal the ticket placed at `check` splits across three surfaces: quotation invariants refuse at the verb, completeness refuses at `render --out`, and `check` re-verifies both against the snapshot for documents that never passed through either. Fourth, a composition the ticket left open: the transplant's destination is a citation of a live modification target, so declaring a transplant forces TET-28's census at the landing site. What none of this touches — deliberately — is content: the tool never decides what counts as a premise, whether a selection is deep enough, or whether a discharge claim is true. Its entire effect is to move the violated property from "asserted by no claim at all" into the evidence ledger, where the existing grounding loop can reach it.","cite":["C2","C3","C7","C9","C13"],"before":"P2","timestamp":1786173500}
9:{"event":"Create","id":"P9","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"## What the document shows

The rendered section sits beside the modification-target section and follows its conventions: labels rather than absolute keys, withdrawn records dropped, and — once the workspace has any claim — an empty section that renders as "none declared" rather than disappearing, because the hole is the finding and an absent section would make the same omission invisible. Per live transplant, the reader sees the donor fact and its extent labels, the destination target, and each premise's selected bytes verbatim in a fenced block with the discharging claim's id beside it. One shipped rule is deliberately inverted here: compose.rs refuses to inline captured output because output is unbounded, but a premise is an author-bounded selection, and inlining it is the entire point — the reader must see the donor's clause on the page, spelled the way the donor's file spells it, next to the claim that answers it, or the section degenerates into opaque pointers. Fenced blocks rather than table cells, because a premise legitimately spans lines and a markdown cell cannot carry multi-line text without escaping it, which would break the byte-fidelity the whole design is built on.","cite":["C11"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786173510}
12:{"event":"Create","id":"P12","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"## Implementation surface, and what this memo does not decide

Every component extends a mechanism already in the tree, and none crosses a doctrinal line: one serde-defaulted per-entry output-length field on the extent record (the compatibility move that added kind and pattern for TET-28, with the same absent-means-ineligible reading — a legacy donor fact is re-looked, never guessed about); one new record kind, counter, and id prefix; a transplants.jsonl written by one CLI/MCP-shared dispatch and shipped by one new line in the snapshot's deliberately-enumerated file list, whose targets.jsonl entry already documents why a record log must ship; one rendered section; one completeness refusal on the render --out path; one snapshot re-verification beside the census one; and the six non-coverage lines. Nothing parses any programming language — the containment check is byte comparison, indifferent to what the bytes mean — nothing is re-run or fetched at check time, and no score or document-level verdict appears anywhere. What this memo deliberately does not decide: the premise-row id spelling and event grammar beyond append-only-with-withdraw; whether span selection should later generalise to ordinary mint-time notes, which stays a Direction item with this feature as its first measurement; and any widening of the trigger toward detecting undeclared transplants, which the measured evidence against prose-reading refusals rules out now for the same reason it did for TET-28.","cite":["C14","C2"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786173544}
14:{"event":"Revise","id":"P4","text":"Two objections were the reason this could not simply be assumed, and both were settled against the capture code rather than by preference. First, strictness: a donor comment wrapped across lines, with `//` or `*` prefixes and indentation inside it, is in fact a contiguous byte substring of a `look` capture, because look_path stores the file's bytes verbatim — no line numbers, no headers, no reflowing. The cost is that the premise appears in the rendered section with its comment syntax intact, which the "never paraphrase" constraint treats as a feature: the reader sees the donor's clause as the donor's file spells it. Stripping the noise would require per-language comment knowledge, the exact coupling the language-agnostic doctrine exists to exclude. The two capture modes that genuinely fight containment — grep outputs, whose lines carry `lineno:` prefixes, and whole-search records, whose output is empty — make `look` of the donor file the natural donor capture, and the refusal's diagnostic should say so; the relation itself stays indifferent to observation kind. Whether the motivating premise itself clears this bar is not left to argument — the next paragraph tests it at the source. Second, soundness: a fact's output is the join of its observations' outputs, boundaries unstored, so whole-output containment would accept text straddling the seam between two captures — bytes never contiguous in anything the author saw. The fix is one serde-defaulted per-entry byte-length field on the extent entry, the same compatibility move that added kind and pattern for TET-28: boundaries become reconstructible, the relation tightens to "substring of a single observation's output", and a donor fact minted by an older build is ineligible with the same cheap remedy — look again.","why":"One sentence asserted how the FORK-94 author had quoted the premise into a fact note — ticket-trail history none of this paragraph's claims (C4, C5) carries. Repl